\subsection{End-to-End Learning: A Negative Result}
\label{sec:gen:dcol}

We test whether end-to-end learning can bypass feature engineering via Deep Contrastive Ordinal Learning (DCOL) with a RoBERTa-large encoder.
Across six configurations, DCOL fails to match feature-based methods: versions v1--v5 suffered training instabilities including embedding collapse, OOM errors, and gradient explosion under various learning rates ($1{\times}10^{-5}$ to $5{\times}10^{-4}$) and margin schedules; the best run (v6) reaches only 64.5\% (vs.\ feature-based 78.2\%) with severe overfitting (train 75\% vs.\ val 64.4\% at epoch 1).
While ordinal contrastive methods such as CLOC \cite{cloc2025} show promise for continuous ordinal targets, the discrete and compressing nature of recursive depth appears to resist end-to-end extraction from raw text.
This suggests that explicit statistical features capture depth-relevant tail behavior that end-to-end models struggle to extract from raw text.

